//
// PROBE: `bund/system`'s path pair — `system.path.split` and
// `system.path.filename`, with their workbench forms.
//
// **These are the pure half of `bund/system`.** They manipulate a string and
// read no filesystem, and the reference does not gate them with `--noio`:
// `unixpath.rs`'s `init_stdlib` takes the command line and never consults it.
// So unlike `filesystem-words.bund` this probe touches no path on disk, and
// F153's trap -- a probe whose meaning depends on its working directory --
// cannot reach it.
//
// **The split keeps the root as a component of its own**, which is the one
// thing about it that surprises: `/a/b` is three components, not two. The
// rest is `unix_path`'s `Components`, measured against the oracle: repeated
// and trailing separators collapse, `.` is dropped except where it is the
// first component, `..` is kept, and the empty path answers an empty list
// rather than failing.
//
// **`system.path.filename` fails where there is no last component** -- `/`,
// the empty path, `.`, `..`, and anything whose last component is `..`. An
// error ends the program, so those five live in unit tests
// (`crates/bund2-stdlib/src/host.rs`) and not here. The message names the
// path and *not the word*, so the stack and workbench forms cannot be told
// apart by it.
//
// **The workbench forms answer on the workbench**, each guarding the side it
// reads -- which is the well-formed shape that F151 records `filename.`
// getting wrong, in a file that otherwise looks the same. Their results are
// left on the workbench for the capture to pin rather than printed, because
// the capture records the workbench after every program.
//
"probe: split keeps the root as a component of its own" println
"/a/b/c" system.path.split println
"a/b/c" system.path.split println
"/" system.path.split println
"x" system.path.split println

"probe: the empty path splits to an empty list rather than failing" println
"" system.path.split println

"probe: separators collapse, a leading dot is kept, dot-dot is kept" println
"a//b" system.path.split println
"a/./b" system.path.split println
"./a" system.path.split println
"../a" system.path.split println
"a/../b" system.path.split println
"/a/b/.." system.path.split println
"a/b/" system.path.split println
"/a/" system.path.split println

"probe: filename is the last component, and trailing separators do not change it" println
"/a/b/c.txt" system.path.filename println
"a/b" system.path.filename println
"x" system.path.filename println
"a/b/" system.path.filename println
"./a" system.path.filename println

"probe: a trailing single dot resolves to the component before it" println
"/a/." system.path.filename println

"probe: the workbench forms read and answer on the workbench, and leave these two" println
"/a/b" . system.path.split.
"/q/r.txt" . system.path.filename.
